# Dashboards

A dashboard brings cards together for an overview, such as power use, room temperatures and open doors. It updates as readings arrive, so people check it many times a day. [Home and rooms](../patterns/home.html) owns household overview dashboards.

## When to use a dashboard

Use a dashboard when:

- people need a quick overview, not a detailed explanation
- the information matters every day and people know what it means
- the data changes often and Home Assistant updates it automatically

Base the dashboard on what the household actually checks.

## When a dashboard is not the answer

Dashboards often:

- leave people to work out what the readings mean
- cannot explain data that needs context
- go out of date as needs change, or when devices are added or renamed
- are hard to make work on every screen

To explain something in depth, such as why the energy bill went up, use a page with a few charts and text.

## Arrange the cards

- Put the most important cards first, such as current power or open doors. Group the rest by room or task.
- Explain a card only where people need it.
- Prefer native cards. A custom card may not support keyboard access, the civica theme or a data table.
- Check the dashboard on a phone, a tablet and a desktop, with long names and many devices. [Using the grid system](structure-grid.html) owns the grid.

### Example: a wall tablet

A wall tablet shows the few readings a household checks most, readable from across the room. Each card has one large reading with its unit and one line of context. The hour still in progress is an outline, not a full bar.

<%=
  hex = ->(id) { govuk_colour(id).fetch('hex') }
  ink, grid, quiet, paper, blue = hex.('black'), hex.('black-tint-80'), hex.('black-tint-95'), hex.('white'), hex.('blue')
  hours = [0.31, 0.28, 0.27, 0.29, 0.30, 0.35, 0.62, 0.88]
  tiles = [['Power now', '1.42kW', 'From the grid'], ['Hall', '18.6°C', 'Target 20°C'], ['Back door', 'Open', 'Since 07:31']]
  svg = []
  svg << %(<rect x="0" y="0" width="360" height="240" rx="14" fill="#{ink}"/><rect x="12" y="12" width="336" height="216" fill="#{paper}"/><rect x="12" y="12" width="336" height="28" fill="#{quiet}"/>)
  svg << %(<text x="22" y="31" font-size="13" font-weight="700" fill="#{ink}">Hughes home</text><text x="338" y="31" font-size="11" text-anchor="end" fill="#{ink}">Tuesday 17 November, 07:40</text>)
  tiles.each_with_index do |(label, value, note), i|
    x = 20 + i * 109
    svg << %(<rect x="#{x}" y="48" width="101" height="78" fill="#{paper}" stroke="#{grid}" stroke-width="1"/><text x="#{x + 8}" y="66" font-size="12" fill="#{ink}">#{label}</text><text x="#{x + 8}" y="98" font-size="24" font-weight="700" fill="#{ink}">#{value}</text><text x="#{x + 8}" y="117" font-size="11" fill="#{ink}">#{note}</text>)
  end
  svg << %(<rect x="20" y="134" width="320" height="86" fill="#{paper}" stroke="#{grid}" stroke-width="1"/><text x="28" y="151" font-size="12" fill="#{ink}">Energy used today (kWh)</text><text x="332" y="151" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end" fill="#{ink}">3.30kWh so far</text>)
  svg << %(<line x1="28" y1="200" x2="332" y2="200" stroke="#{grid}" stroke-width="1"/>)
  hours.each_with_index do |v, i|
    x = 36 + i * 37; h = (v * 40).round(1)
    style = i == 7 ? %(fill="#{paper}" stroke="#{blue}" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 2") : %(fill="#{blue}")
    svg << %(<rect x="#{x}" y="#{(200 - h).round(1)}" width="24" height="#{h}" #{style}/>)
    svg << %(<text x="#{x + 12}" y="213" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#{ink}">#{format('%02d', i)}#{' so far' if i == 7}</text>) if i.even? || i == 7
  end
  rows = hours.each_with_index.map { |v, i| %(<tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="row" class="govuk-table__header">#{format('%02d:00', i)} to #{i == 7 ? '07:40, still in progress' : format('%02d:00', i + 1)}</th><td class="govuk-table__cell">#{format('%.2f', v)}</td></tr>) }
  %(<div class="c-colour-example"><figure style="margin:0 0 20px">) +
    %(<svg role="img" aria-labelledby="tablet-title tablet-desc" viewBox="0 0 360 240" style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:560px;height:auto;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif"><title id="tablet-title">Drawing of a dashboard on a wall tablet</title><desc id="tablet-desc">A landscape tablet shows the Hughes home dashboard at 07:40 on Tuesday 17 November. 3 cards show power now, 1.42kW from the grid; the hall, 18.6°C with a target of 20°C; and the back door, open since 07:31. A wide card shows energy used today in hourly bars, 3.30kWh so far, with the hour from 07:00 drawn as an outline because it is still in progress. The table after the drawing gives every value.</desc><g aria-hidden="true">#{svg.join}</g></svg>) +
    %(<figcaption>A drawing of a wall tablet dashboard, not a screenshot. The household and readings are invented. Entities: <code>sensor.grid_power</code>, <code>sensor.hall_temperature</code>, <code>binary_sensor.back_door</code> and <code>sensor.energy_today</code>.</figcaption></figure>) +
    %(<details class="govuk-details"><summary class="govuk-details__summary"><span class="govuk-details__summary-text">Show the data: wall tablet dashboard at 07:40</span></summary><div class="govuk-details__text"><div class="civica-table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Wall tablet dashboard readings"><table class="govuk-table"><caption class="govuk-table__caption govuk-table__caption--s">Readings at 07:40, 17 November 2026 (invented)</caption><thead class="govuk-table__head"><tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Card</th><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Reading</th></tr></thead><tbody class="govuk-table__body">#{tiles.map { |l, v, n| %(<tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="row" class="govuk-table__header">#{l}</th><td class="govuk-table__cell">#{v}, #{n.sub(/\A\w/, &:downcase)}</td></tr>) }.join}</tbody></table></div><div class="civica-table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Energy used today by hour"><table class="govuk-table"><caption class="govuk-table__caption govuk-table__caption--s">Energy used today by hour (kWh)</caption><thead class="govuk-table__head"><tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Hour</th><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Energy (kWh)</th></tr></thead><tbody class="govuk-table__body">#{rows.join}</tbody><tfoot><tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="row" class="govuk-table__header">Total so far</th><td class="govuk-table__cell">3.30</td></tr></tfoot></table></div></div></details></div>)
%>

Power now is in kW and energy today is in kWh, so they are on separate cards.

<h2 id="bg-dat-09">Keep reports apart from live dashboards</h2>

A fixed report, such as a monthly energy summary, can show one result as a large number or simple shape. Give its date, what it counts, its rounding and its source as real text. Do not use size, area or motion to exaggerate the result.

A live dashboard needs accurate current states. Keep a report's celebratory colours, gradients and claims out of it.

This fixed report states its finding in the title. The text gives the date and what is counted, and the table holds the same facts.

